observation.instruction:
edl.csv is an edit decision list: one row per clip with its source in and out points as SMPTE timecode. job.json gives the frame rate (fps), whether the timecode is drop-frame, and out_point: "exclusive" means source_out is the first frame after the clip, "inclusive" means it is the clip's last frame.
Compute each clip's duration in frames, then the total runtime of all clips in frames, and write the total as a timecode at the same rate in HH:MM:SS:FF notation, counting from 00:00:00:00.
Write runtime.json as {"clips": {"<clip>": <frames>, ...}, "total_frames": <frames>, "total_timecode": "<timecode>"} with integer frame counts.

Your working directory holds edl.csv, job.json. Commands run there in bash, with python3 and git; there is no network.
evaluate grades runtime.json with hidden checks; it scores 1 only if every check passes and 0 otherwise.
You have 32 actions in total. Reply with exactly one JSON action object and nothing else: no prose, no markdown.
Run a command: {"action_type": "exec", "command": "sed -n 1,40p edl.csv"}
Write a file: {"action_type": "write_file", "file_path": "runtime.json", "content": "<the whole file>"}
Finish and get graded: {"action_type": "evaluate"}

